package pages

templ SyntaxFunctionsPage() {
	<h1>Functions</h1>
	<ul>
		<li>Functions are defined using the standard declaration syntax</li>
		<li>Function types can be aliased with a <code>const</code> declaration, e.g. <code>const Callback = fn(status: i32): void;</code></li>
		<li>Function parameters are immutable by default</li>
	</ul>
	<pre>
		<code class="language-ghoti">
			{ `const foo = fn(a: i32, b: u32): u64 {
    // ...
};` }
		</code>
	</pre>
	<h2>Parameters</h2>
	<ul>
		<li>Parameters obey the same syntax rules as the rest of the type system</li>
		<li>
			Without any type modifiers, parameters are passed by value
			<ul>
				<li>All parameters are implicitly constant values</li>
			</ul>
		</li>
		<li>
			A parameter may be passed by const reference by marking it as <code>&amp;</code>
			<ul>
				<li>To call such a function, the call site must also indicate with the <code>&amp;</code> operator</li>
				<li>A const pointer is created with the <code>^</code> operator which the function must also indicate</li>
			</ul>
		</li>
		<li>
			A parameter may be passed by mutable reference by marking it as <code>&amp;mut</code>
			<ul>
				<li>To call such a function, the call site must also indicate with the <code>&amp;mut</code> operator</li>
				<li>A mutable pointer is created with the <code>^mut</code> operator which the function must also indicate</li>
			</ul>
		</li>
		<li>Parameters may have trailing default values (<code>{ "fn(w: i32, h: i32 = w): i32" }</code>), evaluated at each call that leaves the argument off</li>
		<li>
			A parameter's type may depend on an earlier parameter, and the return type may depend on any parameter
			<ul>
				<li>Earlier parameters are in scope for later parameter types and for the return type</li>
				<li>Builtins like <code>{ "@TypeOf" }</code> and type-constructor calls may be used in those positions</li>
			</ul>
		</li>
	</ul>
	<pre>
		<code class="language-ghoti">
			{ `const Box = fn(comptime T: type): type { return struct { val: T }; };

const rewrap = fn(a: auto, b: Box(@TypeOf(a))): @TypeOf(b) {
    return b;
};` }
		</code>
	</pre>
	<pre>
		<code class="language-ghoti">
			{ `const foo = fn(a: &i32): u64 {
    // ...
};

const bar = fn(b: i32): u64 {
    // ...
};

const baz = fn(c: &mut i32): u64 {
    // ...
};

let mut a = 2;
_ = foo(&a);        // Allowed, passed by const reference
_ = bar(a);         // Allowed, passed by value
_ = baz(&mut a);    // Allowed, passed by mutable reference

const b = 1;
_ = foo(&b);        // Allowed, passed by const reference
_ = bar(b);         // Allowed, passed by value
_ = baz(&mut b);    // Illegal, cannot mutate const` }
		</code>
	</pre>
	<ul>
		<li>
			Variadic function parameters are supported to allow for c-interop
			<ul>
				<li>Currently, ghoti does not expose primitives for interacting with this parameter type</li>
				<li>For example, you can declare an extern function like <code>printf</code> from libc as: <code>{ `extern const printf: fn(^u8, ...): i32;` }</code></li>
			</ul>
		</li>
		<li>
			A trailing parameter pack <code>{ "rest..." }</code> takes any number of arguments of any types, each a compile-time-known element
			<ul>
				<li>Read it with <code>{ "rest.len" }</code>, <code>{ "rest[k]" }</code>, and <code>{ "unroll for (rest)" }</code>, and forward it with <code>{ "f(rest...)" }</code></li>
				<li>A slice of the pack forwards only those elements: <code>{ "f(rest[1..]...)" }</code>, <code>{ "f(rest[..2]...)" }</code>, <code>{ "f(rest[a..=b]...)" }</code>, with compile-time bounds within <code>{ "0..=rest.len" }</code></li>
				<li><code>{ "unroll for (rest[1..])" }</code> iterates a slice of the pack the same way</li>
				<li>With <code>{ "if comptime (rest.len == 0)" }</code>, a function can recurse over <code>{ "rest[1..]..." }</code> one element at a time</li>
			</ul>
		</li>
	</ul>
	<h2>Semantics</h2>
	<ul>
		<li>
			There is no function overloading
			<ul>
				<li>This includes operators. There is no operator overloading</li>
			</ul>
		</li>
		<li>Top-level function declarations cannot be marked variable and must be <code>const</code></li>
		<li>
			Functions can have the <code>noreturn</code> return 'type' which signifies that the compiler should not expect a <code>return</code> construct in the function body
			<ul>
				<li>Violating this assumption is a compile time error</li>
			</ul>
		</li>
		<li>The return types <code>noreturn</code> and <code>void</code> cannot have any type modifiers</li>
		<li>You are able to declare local functions (i.e. functions inside of functions) or anonymous functions (i.e. functions passed directly as a parameter to a function)</li>
		<li>
			Closures are implicit as they are in rust
			<ul>
				<li>A closure implicitly captures referenced values by mutable reference if the value is used in mutable contexts</li>
				<li>Non-value objects that can be trivially copied and are not mutated in the closure are copied</li>
			</ul>
		</li>
		<li>
			The <code>move</code> modifier can be placed before <code>fn</code> (e.g. <code>move fn(...)</code>) to force an anonymous closure to capture outer variables by value rather than reference.
			<ul>
				<li>A <code>move fn</code> owns its copies, so a write to one is still there on its next call</li>
			</ul>
		</li>
		<li>Types and <code>const</code> values of the enclosing function are read directly rather than captured, since they are known at compile time</li>
		<li>A closure that holds a reference into its enclosing function cannot be returned from it; mark it <code>move fn</code> to return it by its own type (an <code>auto</code> return)</li>
		<li>The type of a closure is not known to the programmer</li>
	</ul>
	<pre>
		<code class="language-ghoti">
			{ `let mut val = 42;
const closure = move fn(): i32 { return val; };` }
		</code>
	</pre>
	<h2>Generic Functions</h2>
	<ul>
		<li>Functions can accept parameters of type <code>auto</code> or return <code>auto</code> to construct generic functions.</li>
		<li>When a function parameter is declared as <code>auto</code>, the compiler automatically deduces the parameter type at the call site and monomorphizes the function for each concrete type combination.</li>
	</ul>
	<pre>
		<code class="language-ghoti">
			{ `const identity = fn(val: auto): auto {
    return val;
};` }
		</code>
	</pre>
	<ul>
		<li>
			Writing <code>auto</code> under pointer, reference, slice, and array levels restricts what it deduces from: each level must match the argument, and <code>auto</code> binds what's left
			<ul>
				<li><code>{ "x: &auto" }</code> takes a <code>{ "&T" }</code> (or a <code>{ "&mut T" }</code>, viewed as constant) and binds <code>T</code>; a plain <code>T</code> value is an error, like it is for a <code>{ "&T" }</code> parameter</li>
				<li><code>{ "&mut auto" }</code> and <code>{ "^mut auto" }</code> need a mutable argument, <code>{ "[]mut auto" }</code> mutable elements, and <code>{ "[3]auto" }</code> an array of exactly that length</li>
				<li><code>{ "mut?" }</code> takes the argument's own mutability, and levels nest (<code>{ "&[]auto" }</code>, <code>{ "^^auto" }</code>)</li>
				<li><code>impl I</code> works in the same positions, checking <code>I</code> against what <code>auto</code> bound to (<code>{ "xs: []impl Area" }</code>)</li>
			</ul>
		</li>
		<li>
			<code>impl Fn(...): R</code> is an <code>auto</code> parameter that only accepts a callable with exactly that signature: a function, a closure, an erased <code>fn</code> value, or a <code>^fn</code>
			<ul>
				<li>Parameter and return types must match exactly; the parameter names are only documentation</li>
				<li><code>auto</code> in the signature deduces from the callable instead (<code>{ "impl Fn(v: T): auto" }</code>, <code>{ "impl Fn(x: auto): bool" }</code>)</li>
				<li>A mismatch is an error at the call, naming the parameter that differs, rather than somewhere inside the generic's body</li>
			</ul>
		</li>
	</ul>
	<pre>
		<code class="language-ghoti">
			{ `const sum = fn(xs: []auto): i64 { ... };      // any slice, read-only
const fill = fn(xs: []mut auto, v: auto): void { ... };
const map = fn(&self, f: impl Fn(v: T): auto): auto { ... }; // f(v) gives the new type
const count = fn(xs: []auto, keep: impl Fn(x: auto): bool): usize { ... };` }
		</code>
	</pre>
	<h2>Generic Closures</h2>
	<ul>
		<li>
			A local function with <code>auto</code> or <code>comptime</code> parameters is a closure like any other: each monomorph reads and writes the locals around it through one environment built where the function is written
			<ul>
				<li><code>move fn</code> gives it its own copies, and the same capture rules as a closure apply</li>
				<li>It may call itself by name, and closures or other generics in the same function may call it</li>
				<li>Capturing a binding declared after the generic function is an error</li>
			</ul>
		</li>
		<li>
			Every generic function is a value of its own type, which holds whatever it captured
			<ul>
				<li>It can be passed to an <code>auto</code> or <code>impl Fn(...)</code> parameter, which may call it at several types</li>
				<li>Where one signature is expected (a <code>fn(...)</code>, <code>{ "^fn(...)" }</code>, or <code>comptime f: fn(...)</code> parameter, an annotated binding, a field, or an array element) it becomes the monomorph for that signature; a mismatched result is an error at the value</li>
				<li>A <code>comptime</code> function parameter only takes one that captures nothing</li>
				<li>It can be returned by its own type through an <code>auto</code> return, under the same escape rule as a closure</li>
			</ul>
		</li>
		<li>
			Parameter packs work the same way: a closure may take its own <code>{ "rest..." }</code>, and a closure inside a pack function can read, iterate, and forward that function's pack (<code>{ "rest[k]" }</code>, <code>{ "unroll for (rest)" }</code>, <code>{ "rest..." }</code>)
			<ul>
				<li>Where a <code>fn(...)</code> or <code>impl Fn(...)</code> signature is expected, a pack closure takes one element per parameter past its fixed ones</li>
				<li>A closure can expand or iterate a slice of either pack, like <code>{ "rest[1..]..." }</code> or <code>{ "unroll for (rest[1..])" }</code></li>
			</ul>
		</li>
		<li>Inside a generic function, <code>{ "@fnCtx()" }</code> names the generic itself, so each recursive call instantiates for its own arguments</li>
		<li>A generic method can also be called as <code>{ "Type.method(obj, args...)" }</code>, or through a value of it, with <code>obj</code> as the receiver</li>
	</ul>
	<pre>
		<code class="language-ghoti">
			{ `const apply = fn(g: auto, v: i32): i32 { return g(v); };
const make_adder = fn(off: i32): auto {
    return move fn(x: auto): i32 { return @as(i32, @intCast(x)) + off; };
};

let mut calls: i32 = 0;
let scale = fn(x: auto): i32 { calls += 1; return @as(i32, @intCast(x)) * 2; };
let as_fn: fn(n: i32): i32 = scale;     // the monomorph for i32
let total = apply(scale, 4) + scale(@as(u8, 1)) + as_fn(5) + make_adder(10)(@as(i64, 1));` }
		</code>
	</pre>
	<h2>Builtin Functions &amp; Self Recursion</h2>
	<ul>
		<li>Builtin functions are prefixed with the <code>{ "@" }</code> symbol and are always camelCase</li>
		<li>Builtin functions can run at compile time or runtime depending on the context, and will attempt to lower to the most efficient instructions when possible</li>
		<li>Anonymous functions or closures can use the <code>{ "@fnCtx()" }</code> builtin to obtain a handle to the enclosing function for self-recursion.</li>
	</ul>
	<h2>Calling</h2>
	<ul>
		<li>
			Functions are called in three ways depending on the context
			<ul>
				<li>If calling a top-level, non-struct function, the syntax is simple (<code>{ `func(args...)` }</code>)</li>
				<li>
					If calling a member function, the syntax is similar to other languages (<code>{ `instance.func(args...)` }</code>)
					<ul>
						<li>This requires the function to have a self parameter, as discussed in the struct, enum, and union documentation</li>
						<li>The callee must have mutability that matches the explicit self parameter</li>
					</ul>
				</li>
				<li>If calling a 'static' struct function (one without a self parameter), the syntax uses the dot operator (<code>{ `Struct.func(args...)` }</code>)</li>
			</ul>
		</li>
	</ul>
	<h2>Types</h2>
	<ul>
		<li>
			Function's signatures are their types, including parameter modifiers and the return type
			<ul>
				<li>Note that these signatures do not contain parameter names (this is not supported, even optionally)</li>
				<li>Signatures do include the variadic argument distinction</li>
			</ul>
		</li>
		<li>A function can be declared verbosely by using the type before the declaration function</li>
	</ul>
	<pre><code class="language-ghoti">{ `const f: fn(bool): i32 = fn(b: bool): i32 { ... };` }</code></pre>
	<ul>
		<li>You can also use this to indicate that a function takes a function as an argument</li>
	</ul>
	<pre><code class="language-ghoti">{ `const f = fn(g: fn(): i32): i32 { ... };` }</code></pre>
	<h2>Compile-time calls</h2>
	<ul>
		<li>
			Any function declared with <code>const</code> may be called at compile time, from a <code>const</code> initializer, a <code>comptime</code> block, or a <code>comptime</code> header
			<ul>
				<li>The same function stays callable at runtime</li>
				<li>A parameter its body reads at compile time is inferred to be <code>comptime</code>, so each call must pass a compile-time value for it</li>
			</ul>
		</li>
		<li>
			A call evaluated at compile time has restrictions that runtime calls do not
			<ul>
				<li>IO must not be performed (reading from/writing to disk, via threading, printing to stdout, etc)</li>
				<li>Heap allocations may not be made</li>
			</ul>
		</li>
		<li>A local function can be declared <code>const</code> only if it captures no runtime values; a capturing closure is declared with <code>let</code></li>
		<li>Closures and generic local functions also run at compile time, in a <code>comptime</code> block or a function folded at compile time; a write to a captured binding is kept, as it is at runtime</li>
	</ul>
}
